{
  // The professions of workers: on map improvements and in city buildings.
  //
  // Terminology:
  //   * Profession — the "label" of a citizen busy at a map improvement or at
  //     work in a city building. The label is set AUTOMATICALLY on
  //     assignment of a worker/citizen and is removed on dismissal.
  //     The player does not manage labels manually — the profession is determined
  //     by the object the citizen is assigned to.
  //   * The pseudo-profession "all" ("All citizens") — not a worker label but
  //     a population counter (see the entry with "pseudo": true below).
  //
  // Relation to objects:
  //   * an improvement (data/improvements.json) may have a "profession" field —
  //     the id of the profession that works on it;
  //   * the same field for a city building (data/buildings.json) — the id of the
  //     profession of the citizen working in the building (currently: Library and
  //     Scriptorium — "scientist").
  //   If the field is absent — the object has no profession. An improvement marked
  //   with the "no_worker": true field (infrastructure, for example a harbor)
  //   requires no workers at all: they cannot be assigned to it.
  //
  // Resource consumption by a profession:
  //   It is described NOT here but in the resource data:
  //   * the main variant — the registry data/consumption.json, entries of the kind
  //     { "resource": "<id>|@<group>", "profession": ["<id>"], "amount": N,
  //       "interval": S, "production_bonus": B }.
  //     The key "@<group>" refers to a product group from
  //     data/product_groups.json — any suitable product
  //     from the group is consumed (for example, "@boats" — "Boats" for a fisherman).
  //   * the deprecated variant for single cases — the "consumption" field of a
  //     product (data/products/*.json): { "profession": ["<id>"], "amount": N,
  //     "interval": S, "production_bonus": B }.
  //   production_bonus — an addition to the production multiplier (1.0 = no bonus)
  //   while the required amount of the resource is in storage. If there is not
  //   enough of the resource — the object is NOT stopped, it simply rolls back
  //   to the base multiplier (without the bonus). The bonuses of SINGLE entries
  //   add up, while among GROUP ones the best available one is taken (just one).
  //   See docs.md, the section "Professions and resource consumption".
  //
  // Entry format:
  //   id           — a unique string identifier (snake_case).
  //   name         — singular nominative form: "Farmer", "Miner".
  //                  It is NOT shown as a worker label in the interface: it serves
  //                  as the source name in the planned consumption block
  //                  of the "Resources" tab ("Scientist").
  //   icon         — the icon file name (as for products/improvements);
  //                  it is not displayed anywhere at the moment.
  //   description  — a short description for tooltips (optional).
  "professions": [
    // THE PSEUDO-PROFESSION — the top of the hierarchy: it covers ALL citizens
    // of the city — both the idle ones and the busy ones (including workers at
    // improvements with other professions and city workers in buildings). No
    // improvement may refer to it (the "profession" field in improvements.json
    // and buildings.json). Consumption is recorded
    // for it in data/consumption.json and is written off PER CAPITA: the amount in
    // a rule is PER ONE citizen, the total write-off per tick =
    // amount * total_population. "pseudo" is the marker that this is not a worker
    // label but a population counter.
    {
      "id": "all",
      "name": "All Citizens",
      "description": "A pseudo-profession that covers the whole population of the city, including employed citizens.",
      "pseudo": true
    },
    {
      "id": "farmer",
      "name": "Farmer",
      "icon": "profession_farmer.png",
      "description": "Works the fields and tends the crops."
    },
    {
      "id": "herder",
